Now the River faced upstream again. "You honored my vulnerable gift," the River said to the Mountain. "You transformed what I gave and returned it multiplied. Now I have gathered new treasures from distant seas—and I offer them to you with the same trust you once received from me."

The River sent upward exactly what it had given before: nearly two-thirds of its precious cargo, the same measure of vulnerability the Mountain had witnessed and honored.

The Mountain understood the profound meaning of this gesture. "You remember," it said quietly. "You do not simply trust me—you mirror the exact courage you yourself showed. This is deeper than gratitude. This is true partnership."

The exchange continued, each becoming both giver and receiver in turn. When the Mountain next released its stored waters, it too gave generously. When the River returned minerals again, it did so faithfully.

Other rivers and mountains watched this dance of reciprocal trust. They learned that partnership deepens not when one party always gives and another always receives, but when both experience vulnerability in turn, and both choose to honor it equally.

The cycle of mutual trust creates something neither could build alone.
